{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3ypvj4lzq4yrsg4kp3a4c36vkve4cxnrsz7vm2gfmh6fa2egovzq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases passed, and I independently confirmed every artifact on disk.\n\n## What was verified\n\n`c_src/` is a single 180-line translation unit (`lib.c`) exporting 7 functions. `cargo check` was already clean, so no compile fixes were needed.\n\n**Phase A** — three artifacts in `translation/`, derived mechanically from the C:\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — all 7 C exports, each mapped to its Rust `#[no_mangle]` definition. No symbol was missing and no C module was untranslated.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 20 rows, one per distinct rejection site (every `return NULL/0/-1` guard, every `== NULL` check, the `default:` arm, the `strcmp(...)==0` gate, and every checked allocation).\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 37 rows over the axes the C actually branches on (`mode` switch, permission bitmask, `count` shape, string shape, integer/overflow shape, all 7 entry points, and build profile).\n\n**Phase B** — 37 tests, all rows passing with thousands of seeded-random inputs each, comparing return value, `stdout` bytes, and heap out-params byte-for-byte.\n\n**Phase C** — 15 tests covering all 20 error rows, each asserting the *same* sentinel and the *same* message, not merely \"both failed.\"\n\n**Phase D** — `nm -D` diff is empty in both directions (7/7); 0 non-libc undefined symbols; verified under 6 configurations (debug/release × default/no-default/all features) via `check_features.sh`.\n\n## The real bug found and fixed\n\n`multiply_with_log(a, b, NULL)` — the C has no null check and **SIGSEGVs (11)**. The Rust's `*log_msg = ...` matched in release, but under `debug_assertions` rustc instruments raw-pointer stores, so the debug build **SIGABRTed (6)** with `null pointer dereference occurred`. Fixed in `src/lib.rs` using `ptr::write`/`ptr::read`, which emit the bare access. Both profiles now fault identically.\n\nThree harness defects that were silently hiding coverage are documented in `FINDINGS.md` and fixed:\n1. Tests preferred `target/release/`, so `cargo test` could exercise the *release* `.so` — the exact blind spot that hid the bug above. Now derived from `current_exe()`, enforced by test `d7`.\n2. The OOM injection for the four `malloc`-failure rows was **optimized away entirely** (LLVM deleted the unused `malloc` loop and folded `is_null()` to false; `/proc/self/statm` showed zero address-space growth). Those rows would have passed without ever reaching the C's error branch. Fixed with `black_box` + real stores, plus an in-child probe that makes the child exit non-zero if `malloc` doesn't actually fail.\n3. Row E12 needs `malloc(40)` to succeed while `malloc(64)` fails; a single drain couldn't produce that in a multi-threaded process (secondary arena). The child now drains both chunk classes and releases two blocks from deep in the heap. This incidentally cross-checks `sizeof(Result) == 40` between C and Rust.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (only `c_src/build/` was created, as instructed)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1895,"cache_read_input_tokens":225543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1895,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"603139dd-a66f-4d7a-9592-5ebc2faba3d7","uuid":"a577bf55-84eb-4f72-b9c3-cb36f26fd9fe","timestamp":"2026-08-28T00:12:44.962Z"}
